<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Vyatka Land</h1><p class="st-meta">1181 CE – 1489 CE (approx.) · capital Khlynov</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A remote self-governing land of Russian settlers on the Vyatka river, famous for river pirates and finally conquered by Moscow in 1489.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a> and other Russian lands moved into the Vyatka basin among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udmurts">Udmurt</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> peoples. A late local tale dates their arrival to 1181, but this is traditional and uncertain. Their main town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirov">Khlynov</a>, later Kirov, is first reliably mentioned in the 14th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Vyatka had no princely dynasty for most of its history and was run by assemblies and elected leaders, much like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic</a>. Its people farmed, trapped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and joined the ushkuiniki, river raiders who attacked towns along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kama-river">Kama</a>. It was at times subject to the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-tatars">Kazan Tatars</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> sent a large army against Vyatka in 1489. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirov">Khlynov</a> surrendered, its leading men were executed or deported, and the land was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>. Its later history is that of an ordinary Russian province.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vyatka-land.jpg" alt="Vyatka Land" width="320" height="236"><figcaption>Image: Koryakov Yuri · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rus-1389.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1181 CE – 1489 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khlynov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khlynov Republic, Vyatka Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1335&amp;focus=vyatka-land" aria-label="Vyatka Land on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5648 186 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vyatka Land lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5664 460h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5968 442h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5968 442h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1335&amp;focus=vyatka-land">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
